The Lord of His Ruin

♫2360978183

The velvet was green and the sunflower bright,  
When the prince of the wit stepped into the light.  
He conquered the salons of London’s high breed,  
With a epigram sharp as a flashing rapiere.  
He was Oscar the golden, the monarch of prose,  
Who wrapped the dark world in the leaf of the rose.

He wrote of Dorian, beautiful, cold,  
Whose canvas grew rotten as he grew unsold.  
He wore his bright genius like silk on his arm,  
And held the whole city in thrall to his charm.  
Until the dark evening his destiny spoke,  
And the brilliant illusion completely awoke.

